 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_Svk7s3qn6mTBnm4G",
   "name": "Exponential Functions & Graphs",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Exponential Functions & Graphs"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is an exponential function?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An **exponential function** is of the form $f(x)=a^{x},a>0$\n- Its **domain** is the set of **all real numbers**\n- Its **range** is the set of **all positive real numbers**\n- An important exponential function is $f(x)=e^{x}$\n\n  - e is the mathematical constant $2.718281\\dots$\n  - See the following note on \"$e$\" for more details"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the key features of exponential graphs?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The graphs have a $y$**-intercept** at `open parentheses 0 comma space 1 close parentheses`\n\n  - Because  $a^{0}=1$\n- The graph** will always** pass through the **point **`open parentheses 1 comma space a close parentheses`\n\n  - Because  $a^{1}=a$\n- The graphs **do not intersect the **$x$**-axis**\n\n  - The graphs have **a horizontal asymptote** at the *x*-axis $y=0$\n- The graphs **do not have any minimum or maximum points**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/07/6.1.1-Exponential-Functions-fig1.png",
   "file": "assets/f3129cff5153-6-1-1-exponential-functions-fig1.png",
   "alt": "Exponential Functions fig1",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **When **$a>1$\n\n  - For $x<0$ the higher value of $a$ is the “lower” graph\n  - Where $x>0$ the higher value of $a$ is the “higher” graph ![Exponential Functions fig3, A Level & AS Maths: Pure revision notes](assets/6a9b897d6ece-6-1-1-exponential-functions-fig3.png)\n- **When **$0<a<1$\n\n  - Where $x<0$ the higher value of $a$ is the “lower” graph\n  - Where $x>0$ the higher value of $a$ is the “higher” graph ![6-1-1-notes-fig4](assets/117edd01025f-6-1-1-notes-fig4.png)\n- **When** $a=1$\n\n  - The graph is a horizontal line through $y=1$\n  - Because  $1^{x}=1$  for all values of $x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "On the same set of axes, sketch the graphs of  $y=3^{x}$  and  $y=4^{x}$.\n\n*Both graphs will have the 'typical' *$y=a^{x}$* exponential shape for the *$a>1$*case*\n\n> $y=4^{x}$* will be the 'lower' graph for *$x<0$*,and the 'higher' graph for *$x>0$\n\n> *Both graphs go through *`open parentheses 0 comma space 1 close parentheses`* and have an asymptote at the *$x$*-axis*\n\n![jAyO5R1a_picture1](assets/766b19f9d6e1-jayo5r1a-picture1.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_bSVNXtwhvFqkxn6z",
   "name": "\"e\"",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "\"e\""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is e, the exponential constant?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $e$  is one of the most important **mathematical constants**\n\n  - $e=2.718281...$\n  - $e$ is an **irrational** number\n  - `straight f open parentheses x close parentheses equals straight e to the power of x`**  **is often referred to as '**the exponential function**'\n- As with other **exponential graphs**, $y=e^{x}$ ![e Notes fig1, A Level & AS Maths: Pure revision notes](assets/31a3bbcb7f0f-6-1-3-e-notes-fig1.png)\n\n  - passes through `open parentheses 0 comma space 1 close parentheses`\n  - has the $x$-axis as an asymptote"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Why is e so important?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $y=e^{x}$ has the particular **property** that  $\\frac{dy}{dx}=e^{x}$\n\n  - i.e. if  `straight f open parentheses x close parentheses equals straight e to the power of x`, then `straight f to the power of apostrophe open parentheses x close parentheses` is also equal to $e^{x}$\n- This means that for **every** real number $x$, the **gradient** of $y=e^{x}$ is **also** equal to $e^{x}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/07/6.1.3-e-Notes-fig2.png",
   "file": "assets/83e9ac29519c-6-1-3-e-notes-fig2.png",
   "alt": "e Notes fig2, A Level & AS Maths: Pure revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/07/6.1.3-e-Notes-fig3.png",
   "file": "assets/3df93ed751ea-6-1-3-e-notes-fig3.png",
   "alt": "e Notes fig3, A Level & AS Maths: Pure revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "The negative exponential graph"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $y=e^{-x}$ is a **reflection** in the $y$-axis of $y=e^{x}$\n\n  - Note by laws of indices that  `straight e to the power of negative x end exponent equals open parentheses straight e to the power of negative 1 end exponent close parentheses to the power of x equals open parentheses 1 over straight e close parentheses to the power of x`\n- They are of the form `y equals straight f open parentheses x close parentheses` and `y equals straight f open parentheses negative x close parentheses` ![e Notes fig4, A Level & AS Maths: Pure revision notes](assets/484361008669-6-1-3-e-notes-fig4.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "On the same set of axes, sketch the graphs of  $y=e^{x}$,  $y=e^{2x}$  and  $y=e^{-2x}$.\n\n*By laws of indices, *`straight e to the power of 2 x end exponent equals open parentheses straight e squared close parentheses to the power of x`\n\n> $e^{2}>e$*,  so  *$y=e^{2x}$* will be the 'lower' graph for *$x<0$*,and the 'higher' graph for *$x>0$\n\n> $y=e^{-2x}$* is the reflection of  *$y=e^{2x}$*in the *$y$*-axis*\n\n> *All three graphs go through *`open parentheses 0 comma space 1 close parentheses`* and have an asymptote at the *$x$*-axis*\n\n![Aj8jgIxw_picture2](assets/f262f30da9b0-aj8jgixw-picture2.png)"
  }
 ]
}